<PageTitle>Rules — MahjongTutor</PageTitle>

<h1>How American mahjong works</h1>
<p>A plain-language guide to the game as the National Mah Jongg League plays it.
The rules of play are common knowledge — but the yearly <em>card</em> of winning
hands is the League's copyrighted material, which is why you enter your own card
under <a href="/cards">Cards</a> instead of finding one here.</p>

<h2>The goal</h2>
<p>Be the first to complete a set of 14 tiles matching one hand printed on the
card. Every hand is a mix of groups — pairs, pungs, kongs, quints — in specified
suits and combinations.</p>

<h2>The tiles (152)</h2>
<div class="myrack">
    <TileImg TileKind="Tile.D3" /><TileImg TileKind="Tile.B5" /><TileImg TileKind="Tile.C7" />
    <TileImg TileKind="Tile.WN" /><TileImg TileKind="Tile.DR" /><TileImg TileKind="Tile.DG" />
    <TileImg TileKind="Tile.DW" /><TileImg TileKind="Tile.F" /><TileImg TileKind="Tile.J" />
</div>
<ul>
    <li><b>Suits</b> — dots, bams, and cracks, ranks 1–9, four copies each (108 tiles).</li>
    <li><b>Winds</b> — north, east, south, west, four copies each (16).</li>
    <li><b>Dragons</b> — red, green, and white ("soap"), four copies each (12). Dragons pair with suits: green↔bam, red↔crack, white↔dot.</li>
    <li><b>Flowers</b> — 8 interchangeable tiles used where the card shows F.</li>
    <li><b>Jokers</b> — 8 wild tiles. They stand in for <em>any</em> tile, but only inside groups of three or more (pung, kong, quint) — never for a pair or a single.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The deal</h2>
<p>Everyone starts with 13 tiles; the dealer (East) takes 14 and discards first.</p>

<h2>The Charleston</h2>
<p>Before play begins, hands are shuffled by passing tiles:</p>
<ol>
    <li>Pass <b>3 tiles</b> to the player on your <b>right</b>, then <b>across</b>, then <b>left</b>.</li>
    <li>Optionally, if <em>all four</em> players agree, a second Charleston runs in reverse — left, across, right.</li>
    <li>Finish with the <b>courtesy</b>: offer 0–3 tiles to the player across. Each pair trades the smaller offer.</li>
</ol>
<p>Tip: pass tiles that don't fit the hands you're considering — the coach panel
suggests your three weakest tiles.</p>

<h2>A turn</h2>
<p>Draw the top tile of the wall, then discard one tile face-up into the middle.
That's the whole rhythm — draw, discard, repeat.</p>

<h2>Calling a discard</h2>
<p>When anyone discards, the game pauses. If the tile completes a group in your
hand, you may <b>call</b> it — the set moves to your exposed rack and you discard
to end your turn:</p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Pung</b> — 3 of a kind; you must already hold a matching pair.</li>
    <li><b>Kong</b> — 4 of a kind; you hold three.</li>
    <li><b>Quint</b> — 5 of a kind; you hold four (always involves jokers).</li>
</ul>
<p>You can <em>never</em> call a tile to complete a pair or a single — except to
declare <b>mahjong</b> and win. Discarded jokers are dead; nobody may call them.</p>

<h2>Joker exchanges</h2>
<p>If an exposure on the table contains a joker and you hold the natural tile it
represents, you may swap them on your turn — taking the joker into your hand.
The table offers these swaps automatically when you have one.</p>

<h2>Winning</h2>
<p>Complete a hand on the card and declare <b>mahjong</b> — on your own draw
("self-picked") or by calling someone's discard. Watch the card's letter codes:
a hand marked <b>C</b> (concealed) must be built entirely from your rack with no
exposures — so don't call if you're aiming concealed.</p>

<h2>Exposures and dead hands</h2>
<p>Once you expose a group, you've committed: your remaining tiles must still be
able to reach a hand that matches what you've shown. An impossible rack, a wrong
tile count, or a hand that can't exist on the card makes you <b>dead</b> — you
keep playing but can't win.</p>

<h2>Where the coach fits</h2>
<p>The panel on the right suggests passes and discards, warns when a discard
might feed an opponent's exposed hand, and notes calls you could have made —
try the <a href="/drills">drills</a> to rehearse Charleston passes and discards.</p>
